WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.332
Missing threat modeling leaves teams unaware of how checkout flows, refunds, loyalty points, stored payment methods, administrative approvals, or partner integrations can be abused.

2
00:00:12.332 --> 00:00:17.301
Design review should happen before implementation choices become expensive to change.

3
00:00:17.301 --> 00:00:24.785
Unsafe workflows can allow attackers to manipulate business logic even when individual endpoints appear to work as coded.

4
00:00:24.785 --> 00:00:32.687
Payment applications need controls for sequence enforcement, rate limits, approval paths, fraud checks, and transaction integrity.

5
00:00:32.687 --> 00:00:42.794
Weak architectural decisions often involve misplaced trust in internal networks, client-side state, third-party callbacks, or service-to-service communication.

6
00:00:42.794 --> 00:00:47.494
Payment systems need clear trust boundaries and explicit controls at each boundary.

7
00:00:47.494 --> 00:00:54.179
Abuse-case analysis asks what a malicious user, compromised account, insider, or automated bot would do.

8
00:00:54.179 --> 00:00:59.707
That perspective helps teams design safeguards that ordinary functional requirements may miss.

9
00:00:59.707 --> 00:01:03.439
Use threat modeling for payment workflows and trust boundaries.

10
00:01:03.439 --> 00:01:07.200
Include security requirements during design and development.

11
00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:11.132
Review architecture for abuse cases before production release.

